WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.817 --> 00:00:07.150
RYAN LIPPERT: I'd like to
welcome everyone to our Google

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.190
Cloud Next OnAir session.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.940
My name's Ryan Lippert, Product
Manager at Google Cloud.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.950
And I'm going to be joined
by Daniel Lewis from Geotab.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:16.870
Daniel is a Senior Data
Scientist and Research

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:18.940
Specialist for Geotab,
a company which

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.290
works in the telematics space.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.450
That is instrumenting
vehicles to learn

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:25.360
how to optimize fleet
maintenance, routes, costs,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.110
and even city infrastructure.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.110
Now to give you an idea
of how hard that can be,

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.068
Daniel's previous life
was implementing machine

00:00:31.068 --> 00:00:32.409
learning on brain images.

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:34.780
So not exactly a brain
surgeon, but more the person

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.000
telling the brain surgeon
what needs to happen.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.860
So getting around
the side, the theme

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.620
of what we'll be rolling
through is how Daniel and Geotab

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.630
were able to use Google
Cloud's analytic tools

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:47.200
to make their analysis of
their business run easier.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.970
Or in their words, "Don't
sweat the big stuff.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:51.250
Make it Google's problem."

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.115
So let's take a look at the
agenda for this session.

00:00:55.115 --> 00:00:56.490
We'll start by
kicking things off

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.360
with a brief
overview of BigQuery.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.020
Then I'll pass things off to
Daniel for the exciting piece

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.280
of the conversation.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.379
And at the end, we'll
bring things back around

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:07.725
and ask Daniel a few follow-up
questions about his work.

00:01:07.725 --> 00:01:09.100
So let's go ahead
and get started

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.160
with BigQuery, the real time
cloud-scale data warehouse.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.980
Now, when it comes
to analytics, we've

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.320
seen advancements across
different types of databases,

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.200
data lakes, data engineering
tools, business intelligence,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:20.810
and more.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.810
But the reality is that
the data warehouse still

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.850
forms the foundation
for analytics programs

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.388
across most businesses.

00:01:26.388 --> 00:01:28.930
This is important to recognize
because it highlights the need

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.150
to bring users new capabilities.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.580
Not just through new tools,
but through new features

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.620
in their existing
tools, particularly

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:37.990
the data warehouse.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:39.490
Now, when we think
of BigQuery, it's

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.950
helpful to compare and contrast
it with the traditional EDW

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:42.960
environments.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.000
And those were traditionally
complicated to get data into,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.520
restricted to only a
few users, optimized

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.890
for old fashioned
business intelligence,

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.550
optimized for batch data.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.400
They were in need of
constant patching,

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.170
and you needed quite
a few administrators

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.935
for the operational tasks.

00:01:57.935 --> 00:01:59.560
Now, when you hear
from Daniel shortly,

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.510
you'll immediately know
that this kind of tech

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.340
wasn't going to work for Geotab.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.110
Now, when we look
at BigQuery, it

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:08.690
comes at this space from a
very different perspective.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.210
We automate delivery
into the data warehouse.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:13.430
We make insights accessible
through the ability

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:15.530
to scale out that
data warehouse.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.740
We enable ML and AI from
within the data warehouse,

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.900
and BigQuery can do
this all in real time

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.090
while protecting your data
and simplifying operations.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.590
So when you bring
this all together,

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:29.000
it has a dramatic
impact on your business.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.690
And that dramatic impact
is just how much time,

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.470
you're able to spend generating
analysis and insights.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.110
When Daniel talks about
not sweating the big stuff,

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.490
it's a lot of what you
see in this gray area

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:41.280
from traditional
data warehouses.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.010
Difficult people,
intensive processes.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.050
They're about technology
and not about insights.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:48.873
Now from Google
Cloud's perspective,

00:02:48.873 --> 00:02:50.790
we think the hard part
is the applied analysis

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.000
and the insights our customers,
like Geotab, are generating.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.530
We're happy to handle the
backend technology part

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.400
and let customers focus on
analyzing the specific domains

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.330
that they know like nobody else.

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.780
Now, before I pass
things off to Daniel,

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.320
I'd like to quickly highlight
a unique feature of BigQuery

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.300
that, when combined
with BigQuery ML,

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.700
helps make some of
Geotab's analysis possible.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.750
And that's BigQuery GIS.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.510
Now, many people
know Google's long

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.520
been an innovator and
a leader in geodata

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:21.620
with more than a billion people
using Google Maps each month.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.010
With BigQuery GIS,
Google Cloud makes

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:28.070
it possible to do core GIS
functions directly in BigQuery

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:30.080
with familiar SQL statements.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.050
So with that, I
finished off serving up

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.880
the appetizer for
this session, and we

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.797
can move on to the main
course that everyone's

00:03:35.797 --> 00:03:36.545
been waiting for.

00:03:36.545 --> 00:03:38.170
I'm going to pass
things off to Daniel,

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:41.080
who's going to take you through
how he and Geotab have put

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.390
Google's technology to
work in building out

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.157
an impressive business.

00:03:45.157 --> 00:03:46.240
DANIEL LEWIS: Thanks Ryan.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.203
So at Geotab we make
Internet of things devices.

00:03:49.203 --> 00:03:50.620
These little
hardware devices that

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.590
plug into the OBD-II port
in vehicles to enable smart

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:54.640
telematics solutions.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.410
I'm talking about
telematics solutions

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:58.660
generally that's for fleets,
and these little devices

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:00.430
are used to track
those vehicles.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.435
And help companies
make better decisions

00:04:02.435 --> 00:04:04.060
for managing their
individual vehicles,

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.100
and ultimately
operational efficiencies

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.272
across their entire fleet.

00:04:07.272 --> 00:04:09.480
So we got a pretty small
data science team at Geotab,

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.680
though it's grown significantly
in the last couple of years.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.340
But we're able to do so
much for our customers

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.965
because we can rely
on Google to handle

00:04:14.965 --> 00:04:17.950
all of the big backend stuff,
and allow us to really focus on

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:20.170
spending time to solve
our customer's business

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.520
problems and our own
internal business problems.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:24.187
So today we're going
to talk about going

00:04:24.187 --> 00:04:26.560
from all of those individual
telematics devices,

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.140
and the customer level to
how you can bubble that

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.120
up using Google's
awesome scalable

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.860
backend tools to
produce globally

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.050
scalable analytics leveraging
the whole Google Cloud

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:38.170
ecosystem of tools.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:39.638
So as of today,
we've got devices

00:04:39.638 --> 00:04:41.430
installed in more than
two million vehicles

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.060
around the world.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.602
And as I'm talking, you can look
at this really beautiful map

00:04:44.602 --> 00:04:49.050
in the background, which shows
just a single day of vehicle

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.210
coverage in North America
from our connected devices.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.390
So these devices are
constantly streaming data back

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.950
about the vehicle's position,
its speed, its acceleration,

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:00.160
the RPMs, engine
status information,

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.040
cooling temperature, oil
temperature, fault codes.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.540
There's an onboarding
accelerometer.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:05.670
We're going to be
adding a gyroscope.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.180
And just tons of information
constantly streaming back.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:11.493
Now, server side, this results
in over 40 billion data points

00:05:11.493 --> 00:05:13.910
per day, which is just terabytes
of information at our end

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.600
is constantly streaming.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.340
So managing the
streaming process alone,

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.478
that's already a huge
scalability challenge.

00:05:19.478 --> 00:05:21.270
It's one that we solve
with Google's tools,

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:22.830
among many other problems
that we've encountered.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.930
But due to time
limitations today, we're

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.462
just going to
focus on what we do

00:05:26.462 --> 00:05:28.920
after we get all of that data,
which is where my job really

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.128
comes in and the part that
I get really excited about

00:05:31.128 --> 00:05:33.540
and find really interesting.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.540
So what do we actually
do with all of this data?

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.330
These 40 billion data
points that we're

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:38.450
getting every single day.

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.448
So 40 billion data
points is really great.

00:05:40.448 --> 00:05:40.990
It's awesome.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:42.657
I love working with
that amount of data,

00:05:42.657 --> 00:05:44.770
but just the data sitting
there is not actionable

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:46.540
in any way for anybody.

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:49.150
No human can go in and just you
look through that information

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:51.383
and try to parse in, get
anything actionable out

00:05:51.383 --> 00:05:52.550
of it just by looking at it.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:54.310
So what we want to
provide to our customers

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:55.240
are things like solutions.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:57.365
Something that actually
solves the business problem

00:05:57.365 --> 00:05:58.040
using that data.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.740
We do a lot of
things focused around

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:00.920
a few different key areas.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:03.040
Some of them that are really
interesting for our fleet

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:04.930
customers are predictive
maintenance solutions,

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.510
preventing breakdowns
before they happen,

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.270
predicting battery failures,
that kind of thing.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.280
These roadside breakdowns
are very expensive.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.655
We want to use all this data
that's coming off the engine

00:06:12.655 --> 00:06:13.970
to prevent that from happening.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:16.303
We do things like artificial
intelligence driven smarter

00:06:16.303 --> 00:06:17.080
fleet management.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.960
And today's focus is going to
be more on the broader network

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.870
scale of things, so Smart City
Analytics and solutions to make

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:25.090
less congested and just
generally more efficient

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:26.330
cities around the world.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:29.752
Which is a win-win for
everybody involved.

00:06:29.752 --> 00:06:31.210
So why do we care
about congestion?

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.040
It's a really big problem.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.402
If you're looking at
just North America,

00:06:35.402 --> 00:06:36.860
congestion caused
urban Americans--

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:38.860
so not even all of America,
just urban regions--

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:42.910
to travel an extra 8.8 billion
hours-- this was in 2019--

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:45.970
for a congestion cost
$166 billion dollars.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:47.420
So it's a bit of a problem.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:48.510
And these are just
numbers for America.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:50.080
It's an even bigger
problem globally,

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:51.747
so we definitely
wanted to use something

00:06:51.747 --> 00:06:54.970
to solve this, or make it a
little bit better if we can.

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:58.340
Now, there's 20,000
cities in America.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.438
So most of them
are really small,

00:07:00.438 --> 00:07:01.730
but it's still a lot of cities.

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:03.140
And there are multitudes
more around the world.

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:05.020
So it seems maybe a little
bit intractable to start

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:07.062
thinking about how we can
optimize infrastructure

00:07:07.062 --> 00:07:10.060
across 20,000, 30,000,
100,000 cities,

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:12.170
but little small improvements
really do matter.

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:12.795
They can scale.

00:07:12.795 --> 00:07:14.710
You can make these
little bit baby changes

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:17.050
and across hundreds of
thousands of individuals,

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:19.870
you can wind up with really
big cumulative impacts.

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:21.683
So just an example
in San Francisco.

00:07:21.683 --> 00:07:23.350
I looked up the numbers
there, and we've

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.840
got a few 100,000
people entering

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:28.115
cars in San Francisco
every single day

00:07:28.115 --> 00:07:28.990
on their way to work.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:31.573
And if they have to stop at only
five individual intersections

00:07:31.573 --> 00:07:35.230
in the city, and we're somehow
able to shave three seconds

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:36.666
off their average
stop time, we're

00:07:36.666 --> 00:07:39.750
going to save 375 hours of
idling every single day,

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.800
giving each commuter an
hour of their life back.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.370
This is great for the commuter,
it's great for the city.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.412
Getting more people through
with less congestion.

00:07:46.412 --> 00:07:48.780
Less impacts on the environment.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:50.570
It's a win-win for
everybody involved.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:52.070
In order to improve
anything though,

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:54.022
we need to know what
the current reality is.

00:07:54.022 --> 00:07:55.730
We need to know what
the current state is

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:57.560
so that we can push
forward in a direction

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:58.490
that we want to improve in.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:07:59.865
We have to start
with data and we

00:07:59.865 --> 00:08:01.670
have to start with
a lot of it, as you

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:02.850
saw from our previous slide.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:04.190
So here's an example
of the type of thing

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:06.030
that we might want to
produce for a city.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:08.900
So on the left there you can
see a heat map of downtown San

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:10.988
Francisco, and the
heat map is just

00:08:10.988 --> 00:08:13.280
colored by the average wait
time in seconds for a given

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:14.753
hour of the day in the city.

00:08:14.753 --> 00:08:17.420
Now, imagine that you could just
zoom into any city in the world

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:19.253
and access this kind
of detailed information

00:08:19.253 --> 00:08:21.350
for every single
intersection in that city

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:23.480
without any kind of
infrastructure changes.

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:25.190
No camera
installation, no strips

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:27.320
to lay down, no trenches
to dig and put strips in.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:28.970
No one's standing at the
corner with a clipboard

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:31.070
and a stopwatch, which
in talking to cities,

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:32.595
amazingly this still happens.

00:08:32.595 --> 00:08:34.970
Imagine you could just get
all this information instantly

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:37.200
from all of the cars that
are already driving around.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.907
You don't have to do anything.

00:08:38.907 --> 00:08:40.740
This is really what
we're trying to achieve,

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:42.049
and this is what you
can do when you leverage

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:44.132
Google Cloud because of
the volumes of information

00:08:44.132 --> 00:08:46.970
that we're able to
handle with that backend.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:49.460
So to achieve this goal in
this particular talk here,

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:51.140
we're going to restrict the
scope a little bit to something

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.820
a little bit more
manageable, and that's

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:55.070
going to be focusing on signal
controlled intersections.

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:56.930
So these are the things that
the city can tweak immediately

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.150
to get some short-term
feedback on impacts,

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:03.145
and iteratively move
towards a better solution.

00:09:03.145 --> 00:09:04.770
So this is the kind
of thing that we're

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.370
thinking about when we're
talking about traffic light

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:07.110
optimizations.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.547
Going in and removing a stop
sign, or adding a stop sign,

00:09:09.547 --> 00:09:11.880
or adding or removing lanes,
adding dedicated right turn

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:12.780
lanes.

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:14.400
Coming up with
diamond interchanges,

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:15.030
these kind of things.

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:17.280
Total infrastructure changes
are things that you definitely

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:19.155
have to consider, and
they have large impacts

00:09:19.155 --> 00:09:20.130
on traffic congestion.

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.422
But they're not something
that can be easily or cheaply

00:09:22.422 --> 00:09:23.550
manipulated or changed.

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:25.680
With traffic signals,
you can make improvements

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:26.580
with just the lights though.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:27.720
If we're looking at
something simple,

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.550
like a North-South or
East-West direction

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.168
intersection like
we've got here,

00:09:31.168 --> 00:09:33.210
how much green do you
allocate to each direction?

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:34.502
That's an optimization problem.

00:09:34.502 --> 00:09:36.420
If you're going
to a 72nd, 142nd,

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.045
whatever it is life
cycle time, there's

00:09:38.045 --> 00:09:39.795
only a certain amount
of green that can be

00:09:39.795 --> 00:09:41.050
allocated in either direction.

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:42.090
If you give it more
in one direction,

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:43.923
it has to be taken from
the other direction,

00:09:43.923 --> 00:09:45.950
and that has impacts
upstream and downstream.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.700
So how do you actually
know what's better?

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.367
And is the problem
at this intersection,

00:09:49.367 --> 00:09:51.640
or is it upstream or downstream
of this intersection?

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:53.640
On a particular
day, is it because

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.470
of the normal cyclical
traffic flows,

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:57.280
or is it because of an
accident, et cetera?

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.155
We have a lot of data
to be able to determine

00:09:59.155 --> 00:10:01.823
what the optimal light timing
actually is for any given

00:10:01.823 --> 00:10:04.240
intersection, and what the
impacts would be on the network

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:05.330
if we were to change it.

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:06.390
So that's the type of
question that we're

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:07.682
trying to answer with our data.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.788
However, streaming a lot of
data is very, very expensive.

00:10:12.788 --> 00:10:14.330
So it's getting
cheaper all the time.

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:16.413
And when you're talking
about millions of vehicles

00:10:16.413 --> 00:10:19.030
constantly streaming data,
those little bits of cost

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:20.158
add up pretty quickly.

00:10:20.158 --> 00:10:21.700
We can't do continuous
data sampling.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:24.683
We've got real devices,
real hardware limitations,

00:10:24.683 --> 00:10:26.350
with local storage
limitations, as well,

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:27.400
real data transfer costs.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:29.860
We need to figure out what
the ideal sample rate is.

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:31.288
So what's the ideal
sample rate so

00:10:31.288 --> 00:10:33.580
that you don't miss, for
example, going around corners.

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:34.600
As you go around
a corner, there's

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:35.870
a lot of different
things happening.

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:37.120
We don't want to miss
the corner, et cetera,

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:38.410
so we had a higher
sample frequency

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.910
than if you're just going in a
straight line down a freeway,

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:41.540
for example.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:44.230
If we were to constantly drop
points every tenth of a second

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:45.970
or something, we're
bringing back a lot

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:47.290
of redundant
information because we

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:49.370
know you're just going in a
straight line down the highway.

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:50.470
If you have a
uniform sample rate,

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:52.820
you can't really solve both of
those problems simultaneously.

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:54.290
If you crank up the
sample rate, especially

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:55.930
if you're catching
those corners,

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:58.472
you're going to be sending back
way more information that you

00:10:58.472 --> 00:11:01.443
need in areas that you
don't actually need it.

00:11:01.443 --> 00:11:03.860
We solve this at Geotab with
something called Curve Logic.

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:05.940
It's a simple but
very elegant idea

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.940
that we use to minimize
the data transfer costs.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.800
We've got a couple patents
on it listed there.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:11.160
And the basic idea
is really simple.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:12.830
You can watch the cool
animation there on the right

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:13.670
while I describe it.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:16.700
But essentially, you just
have a high frequency sampling

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:18.625
on the actual hardware
device, and then that's

00:11:18.625 --> 00:11:20.000
stored in a local
storage buffer.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.250
Before you send that back,
you go through that buffer

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:24.625
and you choose points from
the sequence of high frequency

00:11:24.625 --> 00:11:25.430
sampled points.

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:28.080
Such that linearly
interpolating between them,

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:30.080
which is what you see
with these red dots there,

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:32.360
no intervening dropped point
would be more than some

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:33.980
chosen error threshold
away from the line.

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:35.480
And that's just a parameter
that you can tweak

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:36.797
depending on your use case.

00:11:36.797 --> 00:11:38.630
And then from the device
to the server, only

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:41.913
sending back those individual
points where we were linearly

00:11:41.913 --> 00:11:42.830
interpolating between.

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:45.270
So only those red points
get sent back to the server.

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:47.480
So we use this technique
to really efficiently send

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:49.438
the minimal amount of
data back to our servers,

00:11:49.438 --> 00:11:51.271
and it enables accurate
trip reconstruction.

00:11:51.271 --> 00:11:53.646
So when you're going in a
straight line down the highway,

00:11:53.646 --> 00:11:55.640
we may only need to drop
down one or two points

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:57.530
from your initial point in
order to tell that you're still

00:11:57.530 --> 00:11:58.370
on that highway.

00:11:58.370 --> 00:11:59.350
Then when you go
on the off-ramp,

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:00.590
we're going to drop a
whole bunch of points

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:02.090
and send those
back to the server

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:06.368
to make sure that we didn't miss
your turn off that off-ramp.

00:12:06.368 --> 00:12:07.910
Now, when we do this
and we come back

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:09.530
and we start looking
at the data in BigQuery

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:11.570
and on our visualizer tools
and that kind of thing,

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:12.950
the first obvious thing
that's going to happen

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:14.360
is now we've got gaps.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:16.843
So on the service
of all these gaps,

00:12:16.843 --> 00:12:18.260
we know in reality,
you would have

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:20.060
had to have driven through
that little green circle,

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:21.140
that intersection
there, in order

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:22.490
to get from one side
of Fulton Street

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.050
to the other side
of Fulton Street.

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:28.080
So how are we going to fill in
all of these gaps server side?

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.645
We know that, because we did
the Curve Logic, that linearly

00:12:30.645 --> 00:12:32.270
interpolating between
those two points,

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:34.562
we would have never been more
dense the error threshold

00:12:34.562 --> 00:12:36.180
that we chose away from those.

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:37.070
So we've got our
two million vehicles

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:38.060
generating billions of points.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:39.643
We know that in order
to fill them in,

00:12:39.643 --> 00:12:41.987
we can just linearly
interpolate between them.

00:12:41.987 --> 00:12:44.070
But with two million
vehicles, billions of points,

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:47.230
how are we going to actually
do that in a scalable way?

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:49.822
We don't want to just linearly
interpolate necessarily

00:12:49.822 --> 00:12:52.030
at a huge resolution in
areas we don't need it again,

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:55.330
but we want to make sure we
don't miss the intersections.

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:57.280
In our particular
use case, because we

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:59.340
are focused on high
quality data production

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:00.840
for these intersections,
we're going

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.570
to drop them at a scale that
allows us to ensure that we've

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:06.070
got coverage in every single
geohash of interest, which I'll

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:08.230
get to later, to do
efficient spatial joins.

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:10.420
In our case, that's a little
bit under nine meters.

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:11.590
So we've got two
million vehicles,

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:14.290
40 billion points a day, we have
to fill in all of these gaps

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:16.000
at a resolution of nine meters.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.250
As you can imagine, that's
a really difficult process,

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:20.230
involving connecting tons
of different systems,

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:21.688
trying to scale
out and parallelize

00:13:21.688 --> 00:13:23.020
the interpretation, et cetera.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:24.200
No, not at all.

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:25.450
Just kidding about that.

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:27.680
With Google you don't need
to sweat that big stuff.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:29.740
End to end, we never
leave the Google ecosystem

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:30.640
for this project.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:32.860
We just make the big stuff
Google's problem entirely.

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:35.050
Let them handle the
hard backend work.

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:36.615
So as you can see
on the slide there,

00:13:36.615 --> 00:13:37.990
with the details
on the right, we

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:40.073
run a daily interpolation
for all of our vehicles.

00:13:40.073 --> 00:13:42.130
And this actually runs
in less than 20 minutes.

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:44.770
All you have to do is work in
a little bit of the simple math

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:49.150
for the linearly interpolated
point dropping between our GPS

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:49.675
points.

00:13:49.675 --> 00:13:51.550
And Google worries about
scaling all that out

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:53.050
on the backend in
a paralyzable way.

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:54.383
We never have to leave BigQuery.

00:13:54.383 --> 00:13:55.970
It's really, really,
really simple.

00:13:55.970 --> 00:13:58.220
So we've been able to minimize
our data transfer costs

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:02.058
and accurately reconstruct
the trips on the server side.

00:14:02.058 --> 00:14:03.600
Now, the reason that
we're able to do

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:05.847
all of this super efficiently
is because of the tools

00:14:05.847 --> 00:14:07.680
that Google has been
continually innovating.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:09.420
And one of the things that
Ryan mentioned at the beginning

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:10.057
was BQGIS.

00:14:10.057 --> 00:14:11.640
This was really a
game changer for us,

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.390
and enabling us to do
all of this scaling.

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:16.080
So July 2018, if I
remember correctly,

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:18.510
is when BigQuery released
native geospatial support.

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:21.120
This allowed us to move away
from relying on JavaScript DDS,

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.872
so those were really
powerful at the time

00:14:22.872 --> 00:14:23.830
when we are using them.

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:26.100
But they don't scale out
as well on the backend

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:27.620
as native BigQuery functions.

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:29.287
So when we started
switching all of that

00:14:29.287 --> 00:14:31.680
over to BQGIS functions, we
were able to really balloon

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:33.480
the scope that we were looking
at for those type of things.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.897
And before we were running it
on a region to region basis,

00:14:35.897 --> 00:14:38.730
and now we run this every
single day for the entire world.

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:40.950
We're constantly
leveraging BQGIS functions

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:44.223
and weaving them into
everything that we do.

00:14:44.223 --> 00:14:46.390
So we've got all of our
data back from the vehicles,

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:48.920
we've reconstructed these trips,
and interpolated the points.

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:50.380
We've got all of the
locations at a very high

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:51.380
spatial resolution.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:52.480
So what do we actually
want to do next?

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:53.950
We have this data, but
what we're trying to do

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:56.440
is find these intersections
so that we can track vehicles

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:56.982
through them.

00:14:56.982 --> 00:14:59.653
Now, this is the tricky part.

00:14:59.653 --> 00:15:01.570
So for a proof of concept
and for the purposes

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:04.870
of this presentation, we used
OpenStreetMap traffic signals

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:07.155
as our point of reference
to look for intersections.

00:15:07.155 --> 00:15:09.280
So an intersection is not
something that's actually

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:12.100
generally well-defined in a
geometric sense in many mapping

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:14.320
solutions, and
particularly in OSM there's

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:17.402
a lot of possible configurations
for traffic lights and ways

00:15:17.402 --> 00:15:18.610
to come into an intersection.

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:20.162
You can see a
sample of them here.

00:15:20.162 --> 00:15:22.120
Sometimes you've got
two-ways and three lights.

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:25.023
Sometimes you've got four-ways
and one light, eight ways

00:15:25.023 --> 00:15:25.690
and four lights.

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:27.120
There's a lot of
different configurations,

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:29.560
and there's no real concept
of the intersection itself.

00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:31.300
Or if you imagine,
like the polygon

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:32.842
that would wrap
around the roads that

00:15:32.842 --> 00:15:34.180
lead into that intersection.

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:37.150
And it's not so easy then to map
these kind of individual ways

00:15:37.150 --> 00:15:39.310
and connected nodes
and lights to something

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:41.147
that has a geographic
representation of what

00:15:41.147 --> 00:15:42.730
the intersection
actually is, in terms

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:44.480
of what a human would
understand, in terms

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.077
of behavior of cars through it.

00:15:46.077 --> 00:15:48.160
So how do we go from some
number of traffic lights

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:50.500
and some number of
ways to a single point

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:53.057
and its associated geometry to
represent that intersection?

00:15:53.057 --> 00:15:54.640
And the way that we
solve that is just

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:58.180
start with a really
simple algorithm DBSCAN.

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:00.430
So we're going to leverage
the DBSCAN algorithm, which

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:01.300
is really simple.

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:02.310
It's a really
powerful algorithm.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:03.940
It's been used for
decades, and it's

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:05.982
really great at finding
local clusters of points.

00:16:05.982 --> 00:16:08.020
However, even for this
really simple use case,

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:10.020
it really quickly becomes
a scalability problem.

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:11.440
So if we look at
the whole world,

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:14.420
OSM has about 1.2 million
traffic light nodes.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:17.290
This leads us to 742 billion
pairwise combinations

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:18.350
of distances.

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:21.040
So if you had data storage
in a 64-bit numpy array,

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:24.125
that's more than 5 1/2 terabytes
of RAM already needed just

00:16:24.125 --> 00:16:26.500
to store the distance matrix
before you started doing any

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:27.950
calculations.

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:31.120
So on top of that, DBSCAN is
not a really easy algorithm

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:33.090
to parallelize, so I'm
starting to sweat a bit.

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:34.300
This doesn't really
seem tractable.

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:35.020
How are we going to do this?

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:37.930
There must be some way that we
can make this Google's problem,

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:41.150
and there definitely is.

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:43.800
So we can solve this actually
pretty easily with Dataflow.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:46.000
Now, Dataflow is great at
having this kind of stuff

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:47.083
in the backend.

00:16:47.083 --> 00:16:48.750
So let's utilize some
of the information

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:50.970
that we have about the
connectivity of these lights.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:52.220
We're not going in blind here.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:54.300
So we know that some of
them are in certain ways.

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:56.170
Those ways are
connected to other ways.

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:57.630
And those ways have lights
on them, some of them

00:16:57.630 --> 00:16:58.358
don't, et cetera.

00:16:58.358 --> 00:17:00.150
So we know something
about the relationship

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:02.483
between the nodes and the
ways and how they're connected

00:17:02.483 --> 00:17:04.000
and the street network.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:04.650
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:06.599
going to utilize those
connectivity relationships

00:17:06.599 --> 00:17:08.819
to reduce the search space
around each light, where it's

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:10.530
only calculating
distances to lights that

00:17:10.530 --> 00:17:12.322
are within some number
of connectivity hops

00:17:12.322 --> 00:17:13.224
to the light itself.

00:17:13.224 --> 00:17:14.849
Plus some other
connectivity parameters

00:17:14.849 --> 00:17:18.337
that we introduce, such as the
type of road and street names,

00:17:18.337 --> 00:17:19.170
that type of things.

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:20.310
These are all
hyperparameters that we

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:22.950
can tune in our approach, but
assume that we've done that

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:26.755
and this leads us to a markedly
reduced number of pre-clusters.

00:17:26.755 --> 00:17:28.380
And with those
pre-clusters we can then

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:29.772
run a paralyzed DBSCAN.

00:17:29.772 --> 00:17:31.980
So when we do this approach,
we get about 1.6 million

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:33.758
pre-clusters to
perform DBSCAN on.

00:17:33.758 --> 00:17:35.550
Each of those are
independent of each other

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:38.125
though, so this means that
we can now parallelize it.

00:17:38.125 --> 00:17:40.000
And on average they have
nine points or less,

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.125
which is a much more
attractive problem for DBSCAN,

00:17:42.125 --> 00:17:44.140
it's a much smaller
paralyzed distance matrix.

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:45.990
So we can basically just
throw it some data for now

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:48.032
and let it handle all the
hard work of paralyzing

00:17:48.032 --> 00:17:50.045
this across all these
1.6 million clusters

00:17:50.045 --> 00:17:50.670
on the backend.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:51.855
Let it scale up
the machines, let

00:17:51.855 --> 00:17:53.240
it worry about how to
distribute the work.

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:54.660
How to collect it after
it, how to stream it

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:55.650
back into BigQuery.

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:58.080
All I had to do is
figure out the approach.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:17:59.790
So we're able to
really easily do this,

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:01.340
and this runs pretty fast.

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:03.810
So now we've identified
700,000 intersections

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:06.030
in the entire world in
less than 10 minutes

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:07.745
using just BigQuery
and Dataflow.

00:18:07.745 --> 00:18:08.620
So this is excellent.

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:10.960
We still haven't left the core
Google Cloud product suite.

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:12.502
So now we can go on
to the next step,

00:18:12.502 --> 00:18:13.703
which is mapping the roads.

00:18:13.703 --> 00:18:15.120
So once we have
the intersections,

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:16.740
we have to accurately
map the roads in order

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:18.865
to accurately track where
the vehicles are actually

00:18:18.865 --> 00:18:21.480
coming from and going to so
that we can get accurate metrics

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:22.830
at a per-intersection level.

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:24.420
And ultimately
chain them together

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:27.180
to get quarter analytics and
a broader network effects

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:29.580
as well.

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:31.580
So in order to efficiently
generate these trips,

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:34.178
we want to be able to do a
very simple geospatial join.

00:18:34.178 --> 00:18:36.470
So if we were to do something
like a simple naive cross

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:38.120
join between all
of our GPS points

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:39.590
and all of the
roads in the world,

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:42.730
and maybe use something like a
BQGIS function like ST_DWithin.

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:45.317
We'd have an absolutely
astronomical number of roads.

00:18:45.317 --> 00:18:47.150
So this is not possible
even for Google yet.

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:49.230
It's far more than
BigQuery can handle.

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:51.105
So there has to be
a better smarter way

00:18:51.105 --> 00:18:53.480
that we can figure out what
road you're on really quickly

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:54.980
in a scalable way
for these millions

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:57.590
and millions of vehicles and
these billions of points.

00:18:57.590 --> 00:19:00.020
And there's a lot of smarter
ways that we could do this.

00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:01.790
And at Geotab we
tend to use Geohash

00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:03.360
for geocoding latitude
and longitude.

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.810
So Geohash is just a
Z-order space filling curve.

00:19:05.810 --> 00:19:07.520
You can think of it, if
you haven't used it before,

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:09.062
something just like
nested rectangles

00:19:09.062 --> 00:19:10.340
that cover the whole globe.

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:12.260
Any two latitude
and longitude pair

00:19:12.260 --> 00:19:14.540
inside one of these
little rectangular boxes

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:17.080
gets assigned a string
which is unique to that box.

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:18.872
And there's a lot of
other mapping systems,

00:19:18.872 --> 00:19:20.530
such as S2 cells,
H3 cells, et cetera,

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:22.420
but at Geotab we
tend to use Geohash,

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:25.020
and it has native supporting
BQGIS, which is great.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:26.905
We don't have to jump
over to JavaScript DDS.

00:19:26.905 --> 00:19:29.030
So this allows us to forget
about the impossibility

00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:31.040
of accurately joining
floats and the impossibility

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:32.165
of doing these cross joins.

00:19:32.165 --> 00:19:35.220
It enables us to do a
very fast straight string

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:36.998
join based on Geohash.

00:19:36.998 --> 00:19:39.540
So that's great, but that then
leads us into another problem.

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:41.900
So if we're going to be doing
the straight join in Geohash,

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:43.970
we have to then know which
geohashes are actually

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:46.730
part of the road network and
which parts of the road network

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:48.770
are associated with
which intersection.

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:50.150
So there are some machine
learning approaches

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:52.070
that we've used, but there are
actually some simpler cleverer

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:54.853
ways that you can do leveraging
the ability of BigQuery scale

00:19:54.853 --> 00:19:56.270
really efficiently
before you even

00:19:56.270 --> 00:19:58.400
have to get into the
machine learning area.

00:19:58.400 --> 00:19:59.210
So the hard part.

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:01.460
Back to the problem, which
is really a trip generation

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:01.940
problem.

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:03.430
In order to generate
trips, again, we

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:04.810
need to know the set
of possible traversals

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:06.393
that is through any
given intersection

00:20:06.393 --> 00:20:09.398
to segment the vehicle path
into intersection-based trips.

00:20:09.398 --> 00:20:11.690
So before we even think about
associating the geohashes

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:14.030
with the ways, we need to
know which ways are associated

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:15.773
with the intersection.

00:20:15.773 --> 00:20:17.440
So as a first step,
we can just pull all

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:19.140
the directly connected ways
out of the intersection,

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:20.720
so anything that's directly
touching or connected

00:20:20.720 --> 00:20:21.720
to a traffic light.

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:23.928
So you can see an example
of that on the bottom right

00:20:23.928 --> 00:20:25.978
at the slide for some
random intersection

00:20:25.978 --> 00:20:26.770
that I just picked.

00:20:26.770 --> 00:20:28.250
However, you can
only see on the map

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:30.290
the adjacent intersections
to the left, right, North,

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:33.000
and South are further away than
those ways that are extending.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:34.280
So we've got these
directly connected ways

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:35.905
but they don't actually
map all the way

00:20:35.905 --> 00:20:37.200
to the next intersection.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:39.080
So if you were to just
base trips on this,

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:41.330
they're not actually going
to be accurately reflecting

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:42.360
the metrics for those vehicles.

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:43.490
They're only going to
be counting vehicles

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:45.050
that are on those
lines, assuming

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:47.175
we've done the geohash
associated with those lines.

00:20:47.175 --> 00:20:49.507
So we miss all of the
intervening information

00:20:49.507 --> 00:20:51.340
from when you left the
previous intersection

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:53.590
until you get to these short
distance approaches here.

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:55.645
So that's something we're
going to have to fix.

00:20:55.645 --> 00:20:57.020
So what we actually
need to do is

00:20:57.020 --> 00:20:58.790
start with all of those
directly connected ways,

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:01.010
and then somehow get all
the ways connected to those.

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:02.810
And then all the ways
connected to those, and those,

00:21:02.810 --> 00:21:03.810
and so on and so forth.

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:05.510
Going backwards until
we either run out

00:21:05.510 --> 00:21:07.982
of ways that are connected or
we hit the next intersection,

00:21:07.982 --> 00:21:09.440
while simultaneously
somehow taking

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:12.110
care not to include side streets
and different types of roads

00:21:12.110 --> 00:21:13.280
and things like that.

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:14.900
So you can see with the code
on the bottom right there,

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:16.680
it's actually pretty easy to
get all the connected ways.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:18.500
It's a really simple
join on the nodes.

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:21.670
There's nothing really
challenging about that.

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:23.580
So this is ultimately
what we want to do.

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:26.890
You can see there's 132
connected ways here.

00:21:26.890 --> 00:21:29.070
So we've got our simple
query to join back and get

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:30.180
the connected ways.

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:32.010
In this particular case, we
know how many hours so we're

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:32.610
good to go, right?

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:33.720
We just keep joining
until we've got

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:35.387
all these ways that
are highlighted here

00:21:35.387 --> 00:21:36.840
for this intersection.

00:21:36.840 --> 00:21:38.850
But of course, it's
never quite that easy.

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:41.160
So we don't know a priori
for any given intersection

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:43.785
how many ways are connected, so
we don't know how many times we

00:21:43.785 --> 00:21:44.830
need to actually join.

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:45.510
So what we're
going to have to do

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:48.090
is look recursively until
we run out of connections.

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:49.860
In order to scale,
we know we want

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:52.830
to do this recursion in
BigQuery and just leverage SQL.

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:54.990
Unfortunately you can't
do recursion in SQL, so

00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:56.190
what are we going to do?

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:58.030
Or can we?

00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:01.180
So I'm super excited about
this and it's really recent.

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:03.635
So in November 2019 BigQuery
released native scripting

00:22:03.635 --> 00:22:05.510
support, which is really
amazing and powerful

00:22:05.510 --> 00:22:07.550
and allows you to do
really quick prototyping,

00:22:07.550 --> 00:22:09.430
experimenting, and iteration
of things that weren't really

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:10.972
possible before
right inside BigQuery

00:22:10.972 --> 00:22:12.710
and right inside the UI,.

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:14.355
So you can run
scripts via API, just

00:22:14.355 --> 00:22:15.730
like a run of the
mill SQL query,

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:16.855
you could run it in the UI.

00:22:16.855 --> 00:22:18.610
It's got all the
basic building blocks

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:20.777
as well that you would want
in a scripting language,

00:22:20.777 --> 00:22:23.593
like conditional execution,
loops, we can mix DML and SQL.

00:22:23.593 --> 00:22:25.510
It gives us some really
powerful capabilities,

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:27.968
and we're going to leverage
all those capabilities to solve

00:22:27.968 --> 00:22:29.647
this connectivity problem.

00:22:29.647 --> 00:22:31.230
So I was really
excited this came out,

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:33.420
and I immediately ran
over to the documentation.

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:35.312
And unfortunately, the
documentation for this

00:22:35.312 --> 00:22:37.020
is super underwhelming,
and they actually

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:39.090
are really underselling
the power and capability

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:39.970
of this tool.

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:41.100
So if you see on the
left there, that's

00:22:41.100 --> 00:22:42.892
the example for loops
in the documentation.

00:22:42.892 --> 00:22:44.907
No, we're never going
to use SQL to increment

00:22:44.907 --> 00:22:47.490
some random variable X and spit
it out at the end of the loop.

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:49.743
This is just not a use
case that would happen.

00:22:49.743 --> 00:22:51.660
This is really the extent
of the documentation

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:53.250
that they have for loops,
so I wanted to do something

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:54.833
a little more fun
to learn it and then

00:22:54.833 --> 00:22:57.780
also showcase the power of it.

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:00.300
So I went and tried to
implement a recursive Fibonacci

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:02.430
generator using nothing
but SQL and BigQuery,

00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:03.888
and the result is
here on the left.

00:23:03.888 --> 00:23:04.596
It totally works.

00:23:04.596 --> 00:23:07.013
I wouldn't recommend doing
recursive Fibonacci in general,

00:23:07.013 --> 00:23:08.490
and definitely
don't do it in SQL.

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:10.530
But the point is that this
is a really powerful tool,

00:23:10.530 --> 00:23:11.460
and the things that
you would think

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:13.460
are not going to be
possible to implement in SQL

00:23:13.460 --> 00:23:16.018
and weren't before, you can now
do really easily in BigQuery.

00:23:16.018 --> 00:23:17.810
And you can copy the
code there on the left

00:23:17.810 --> 00:23:18.630
if you want and run it.

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:19.338
It totally works.

00:23:19.338 --> 00:23:21.570
It's going to get scaling
problems at some point

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:24.537
as it explodes recursively,
but the point is that it works.

00:23:24.537 --> 00:23:26.370
And when we implement
this in a smarter way,

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:28.170
we can solve things like
our connected ways problem

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:29.730
without ever leaving
the SQL environment,

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:32.147
and letting Google handle all
that big complicated backend

00:23:32.147 --> 00:23:32.700
stuff.

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:34.242
So here's some
actually usable codes.

00:23:34.242 --> 00:23:35.700
On the left there
is the final code

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:38.320
that I wrote to actually do this
with a SQL scripted approach.

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:40.110
So what we can do is we
can build a while loop that

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:41.490
just keeps joining
backwards and keeps

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:43.950
joining backwards on way nodes
until we can't find anymore.

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:45.420
So it's a really
simple while loop.

00:23:45.420 --> 00:23:47.212
We can set some break
condition if we want.

00:23:47.212 --> 00:23:50.190
15, 30, 100, whatever we like
in terms of number of connected

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:51.030
hops.

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:53.550
And insert the results of each
step into our reference table

00:23:53.550 --> 00:23:55.130
at the end of a loop iteration.

00:23:55.130 --> 00:23:56.910
And at the start of a loop,
we just join back on it

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:58.820
with the ways and nodes until
the next loop iteration.

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:00.090
We just keep doing this
and we just keep doing this

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:02.340
until we hit our break
limit or until the table

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:03.690
size stops changing.

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:05.610
We know that we've run
out of connected ways.

00:24:05.610 --> 00:24:07.440
So once you bring in the
ability to leverage loops,

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:09.732
this actually becomes a really
simple problem to solve.

00:24:09.732 --> 00:24:11.777
We just take our simple
join and we recursively

00:24:11.777 --> 00:24:13.610
iterate it until we run
out of connectivity.

00:24:13.610 --> 00:24:14.910
And then you can
just lean on BigQuery

00:24:14.910 --> 00:24:16.327
and it's awesome
ability to scale.

00:24:16.327 --> 00:24:18.618
And this whole thing actually
runs in less than an hour

00:24:18.618 --> 00:24:19.690
for the entire world.

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:21.732
So Google handles the
paralyzation on the backend

00:24:21.732 --> 00:24:24.530
really, really well.

00:24:24.530 --> 00:24:27.710
So here's the results of that
in a particular neighborhood.

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:29.450
This is just Oakville
where I live.

00:24:29.450 --> 00:24:31.717
And this has been done
for the entire world,

00:24:31.717 --> 00:24:33.800
so we've got every single
connected way associated

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:35.685
with every single
intersection in the world.

00:24:35.685 --> 00:24:37.850
So this is really fantastic,
and in combination with the data

00:24:37.850 --> 00:24:40.610
flow step that we did before
and the couple of BigQuery step,

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:42.860
we're still under two hours
end to end to do all this.

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:45.318
To get all the connected ways
associated with intersections

00:24:45.318 --> 00:24:48.400
for the entire planet.

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:50.242
So what do we
actually do with it?

00:24:50.242 --> 00:24:52.200
So we have the roads,
but this doesn't actually

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:53.500
solve the problem yet.

00:24:53.500 --> 00:24:55.920
We still need all of
those associated geohashes

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:58.140
and we're back to our
original cross join problem.

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:00.940
Except instead of geographic
points, we now have geohashes.

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:02.760
So we still can't just
join these geohashes

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:05.093
on the roads, that geohashes
that cover the whole planet

00:25:05.093 --> 00:25:06.635
and figure out which
road they're on.

00:25:06.635 --> 00:25:09.360
So we still got 20 billion
geohashes and 18 million ways

00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:10.790
after the reduction step.

00:25:10.790 --> 00:25:13.928
That's still a 386
quintillion row cross join.

00:25:13.928 --> 00:25:15.970
That's still way too much
for BigQuery to handle,

00:25:15.970 --> 00:25:19.140
so again, we need to come up
with a smarter more clever way

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:20.990
to do this.

00:25:20.990 --> 00:25:22.760
Now, Google's really
good at scaling,

00:25:22.760 --> 00:25:24.020
which is the point I'm
trying to drive home

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:24.970
in this presentation.

00:25:24.970 --> 00:25:26.930
So if you can craft a
problem in such a way

00:25:26.930 --> 00:25:28.958
that the hard part
is scaling, at least

00:25:28.958 --> 00:25:30.750
within astronomical
limits, you pretty much

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:32.917
won because Google can just
handle all that for you.

00:25:32.917 --> 00:25:33.900
So let's do that.

00:25:33.900 --> 00:25:35.780
So let's think a little bit
more carefully about the problem

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:38.212
and create it in such a way
that we're stuck on a scaling

00:25:38.212 --> 00:25:39.920
problem, and then just
throw it at Google

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:41.670
and let it deal
with that for us.

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:43.878
So what we can do is actually
break this problem down

00:25:43.878 --> 00:25:45.170
into two primary steps.

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:48.950
First, what we need is actually
a subset of close-by geohashes.

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:51.500
From there we can restrict the
set of geohashes in some way

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:52.917
so those will just
cover the road.

00:25:52.917 --> 00:25:55.040
And then we are back to
our original problem,

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:57.710
where we have [INAUDIBLE] GPS
points, because we can easily

00:25:57.710 --> 00:26:00.700
get geohashes for those
points directly with BQGIS.

00:26:00.700 --> 00:26:01.910
It's just a straight join.

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:03.620
We don't cross join
required anymore.

00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:04.450
And now we've got
something that's

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:05.600
going to work
really well and it's

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:06.980
going to scale really well.

00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:09.620
So we can't just geohash the
nodes that make up the ways.

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:11.180
Those are unevenly
spaced on the road

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:12.140
so we're going to get gaps.

00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:13.765
We could start with
that maybe and look

00:26:13.765 --> 00:26:16.850
at parent nodes and neighbor
nodes, parent neighbor

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:17.902
geohashes, et cetera.

00:26:17.902 --> 00:26:20.360
We're going to have a lot of
edge cases to deal with there,

00:26:20.360 --> 00:26:22.280
and those arrays don't
scale super well.

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:24.130
We know the cross joins are
going to work, so how are we

00:26:24.130 --> 00:26:25.140
going to actually do this?

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:27.480
So what we need to do is make
some sort of search space

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:28.400
ourselves.

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:31.640
We can do this using just
SQL UDFs and BQGIS again.

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:33.170
So all we really
need to start with

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:34.628
is some reduced
search space, which

00:26:34.628 --> 00:26:36.418
we can visualize
as a bounding box

00:26:36.418 --> 00:26:38.210
with some incremental
spacing from one side

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:39.530
of the bounding box to another.

00:26:39.530 --> 00:26:42.448
This gives us basically
a matrix of possibilities

00:26:42.448 --> 00:26:44.240
of lat long points that
we stepped through.

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:47.060
And we convert each of those
into a geohash using BQGIS,

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:48.560
and then only retain
those geohashes

00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:50.710
that are within some
distance of the road.

00:26:50.710 --> 00:26:52.793
We can let Google then
handle the big stuff, which

00:26:52.793 --> 00:26:55.682
is scaling out this approach
to the entire world.

00:26:55.682 --> 00:26:57.890
So this is the result of
our first pass on a randomly

00:26:57.890 --> 00:26:58.477
selected road.

00:26:58.477 --> 00:27:00.560
What we've done is surrounded
this particular road

00:27:00.560 --> 00:27:02.935
with a bounding box, which
you can see on the left there.

00:27:02.935 --> 00:27:04.435
It's very easy to
do just by getting

00:27:04.435 --> 00:27:06.960
the extense of the geometry
and adding some buffer space.

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:09.290
And then we have this
wrapped box around the road.

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:11.510
And then we simply use
BigQuery to generate an array,

00:27:11.510 --> 00:27:12.860
where we step by
an amount smaller

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:14.527
than the geohash
dimensions of interest.

00:27:14.527 --> 00:27:16.250
And I'm taking into
account the distance

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:18.542
from the equator, because
they're not perfectly linear,

00:27:18.542 --> 00:27:19.550
the Earth is not flat.

00:27:19.550 --> 00:27:22.440
And then we geocode these points
of not long into our greater

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:24.920
geohashes and then voila,
we've covered the whole road

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:26.120
with geohashes.

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:28.820
So from there we can now finally
do our ST_DWithin function

00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:30.750
that we were talking
about several slides ago.

00:27:30.750 --> 00:27:32.840
That's just a singular geometry
comparison for each way

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:34.730
and it's potentially
associated geohashes.

00:27:34.730 --> 00:27:39.590
Instead of this massive cross
join, which is impossible.

00:27:39.590 --> 00:27:42.015
So now we've done this
ST_DWithin function,

00:27:42.015 --> 00:27:43.640
and we've got all of
the geohashes that

00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:44.978
are covering the road.

00:27:44.978 --> 00:27:46.520
We get the estimated
road width based

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:47.978
on things like the
number of lanes,

00:27:47.978 --> 00:27:50.000
the geographical
region of interest,

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:54.160
the prescribed maximum amount
of roadways for a given

00:27:54.160 --> 00:27:55.305
municipality, et cetera.

00:27:55.305 --> 00:27:56.930
And we've got some
models that do that.

00:27:56.930 --> 00:27:59.390
And now we've got a wonderfully
reduced set of geohashes

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:01.015
that accurately covers
the road itself.

00:28:01.015 --> 00:28:03.432
So once we parallelize this
a few times with [INAUDIBLE],,

00:28:03.432 --> 00:28:05.270
this actually runs in
less than four hours

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:08.313
for the entire planet.

00:28:08.313 --> 00:28:10.480
So here's an example of
just all of the intersection

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:11.790
roads in Singapore for fun.

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:14.290
And so remember that we never
left BigQuery for any of this.

00:28:14.290 --> 00:28:16.337
I never looked at any
data from Singapore.

00:28:16.337 --> 00:28:17.920
I mean, I picked a
few local examples.

00:28:17.920 --> 00:28:20.170
Figured out how to
best cleverly come up

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:22.960
with a process that leverages
the ability of BigQuery

00:28:22.960 --> 00:28:23.715
to scale.

00:28:23.715 --> 00:28:25.465
And then we're going
from basically a text

00:28:25.465 --> 00:28:27.673
string representing the
geography of a roadway, which

00:28:27.673 --> 00:28:30.280
is stored in BigQuery, without
any direct relationship

00:28:30.280 --> 00:28:33.255
to the intersection, to a set
of all the geohashes covering

00:28:33.255 --> 00:28:34.630
all the roads in
the entire world

00:28:34.630 --> 00:28:37.570
the lead up into a particular
intersection using nothing

00:28:37.570 --> 00:28:38.500
but SQL.

00:28:38.500 --> 00:28:40.270
And we still spend for
this whole pipeline

00:28:40.270 --> 00:28:42.560
only around six hours
from end and to run.

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:45.100
So all in a day's work we've
gone from some simple text

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:47.155
strings to
representative geohashes

00:28:47.155 --> 00:28:48.280
covering the entire planet.

00:28:50.672 --> 00:28:52.130
So now we can
actually go and start

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:53.730
generating metrics
for the cities.

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:55.605
And this was the purpose
of doing all of this

00:28:55.605 --> 00:28:56.750
to begin with.

00:28:56.750 --> 00:28:59.008
So since we have intersections
with labels and all

00:28:59.008 --> 00:29:01.550
of the associated geohashes now,
their centers, their bounds,

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:04.505
et cetera, all we have to do
is geocode our vehicle path GPS

00:29:04.505 --> 00:29:07.130
points into geohashes, and then
join back on this intersections

00:29:07.130 --> 00:29:08.480
table that we created.

00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:11.450
From there we can easily
assign each portion of a trip

00:29:11.450 --> 00:29:13.502
to the intersection that
it passes through next.

00:29:13.502 --> 00:29:14.960
And from there it's
a simple matter

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:17.360
of tracking things like
speed, the number of stops,

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:19.460
the idle time, the
idle time per stop,

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:22.157
the total distance traveled from
the point from which you first

00:29:22.157 --> 00:29:23.990
stopped, which we can
use to infer cue links

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:24.920
and things like that.

00:29:24.920 --> 00:29:26.628
To pull out really
any metric of interest

00:29:26.628 --> 00:29:29.310
and subsequently aggregate up
to the time period of interest.

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:32.180
So if you imagine, you're
traveling out of your driveway

00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:33.467
and you get onto the road.

00:29:33.467 --> 00:29:35.050
And at some point
on that road, you're

00:29:35.050 --> 00:29:36.080
going to hit an
intersection where

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:38.640
you have to make a turn decision
or a straight through decision.

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:40.250
So the point from which
you leave that driveway

00:29:40.250 --> 00:29:42.375
and to the point which you
get to that intersection

00:29:42.375 --> 00:29:44.672
is going to be one subset
of your total trip.

00:29:44.672 --> 00:29:46.880
That subset has a bunch of
metrics associated with it

00:29:46.880 --> 00:29:48.588
and get associated
with that intersection

00:29:48.588 --> 00:29:49.730
that you then pass through.

00:29:49.730 --> 00:29:51.680
From the moment that you
leave that intersection,

00:29:51.680 --> 00:29:53.630
we start counting again
towards the next intersection

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:55.080
that you experience,
and so on and so forth.

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:56.660
Then we break up
all of your driving

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:58.785
into these little trip
segments that are associated

00:29:58.785 --> 00:30:00.170
with a given intersection.

00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:02.330
An example there on
the right there is just

00:30:02.330 --> 00:30:03.990
for the region of Toronto.

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:05.605
So using that
aggregated information,

00:30:05.605 --> 00:30:06.980
we can now really
easily generate

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:09.600
all sorts of insights for any
city where we've got vehicles.

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:11.030
And as you saw in one of
our first slides there,

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:13.058
that's pretty much any
city in North America.

00:30:13.058 --> 00:30:14.600
And we're expanding
globally as well.

00:30:14.600 --> 00:30:16.340
So you can see some cool
examples on the slide here.

00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:18.230
So on the left, we've
got the average trip

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:19.880
volume for heavy duty vehicles.

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:21.380
You can see that
they tend to prefer

00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:22.797
certain quarters
through the city,

00:30:22.797 --> 00:30:24.350
and this is the
Las Vegas region.

00:30:24.350 --> 00:30:26.080
On the top right, from
Las Vegas as well,

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:28.455
we've got a quarter analysis
heat map of individual trips

00:30:28.455 --> 00:30:30.160
through a sequence
of intersection.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:32.035
And this essentially
shows you the propensity

00:30:32.035 --> 00:30:34.670
to have to stop at any given
intersection along the quarter.

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:36.930
On the bottom right is just
a simple heat map example

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:38.180
showing the
percentage of vehicles

00:30:38.180 --> 00:30:40.790
that have to stop at any given
intersection based on the turn

00:30:40.790 --> 00:30:41.640
direction.

00:30:41.640 --> 00:30:44.182
If you'll look closely, you can
see some interesting patterns

00:30:44.182 --> 00:30:45.920
there, like rush
hour times, depending

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:48.087
on the direction at certain
times a day, around 8:00

00:30:48.087 --> 00:30:49.830
AM and around 5:00 and 6:00 PM.

00:30:49.830 --> 00:30:52.670
And at the bottom there, you can
actually see two right turns.

00:30:52.670 --> 00:30:54.920
One of which has a
significantly higher propensity

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:56.045
to stop than the other one.

00:30:56.045 --> 00:30:57.462
And if you go and
look on the map,

00:30:57.462 --> 00:31:00.267
it's because that turn direction
has a dedicated right turn lane

00:31:00.267 --> 00:31:01.350
and the other one doesn't.

00:31:01.350 --> 00:31:02.360
So the one where you
don't have to stop

00:31:02.360 --> 00:31:03.700
has a dedicated turn lane.

00:31:03.700 --> 00:31:04.820
And those things
like that are really

00:31:04.820 --> 00:31:06.160
easy to pick out
of these things,

00:31:06.160 --> 00:31:08.077
and really validate the
infrastructure changes

00:31:08.077 --> 00:31:09.110
that you've been making.

00:31:09.110 --> 00:31:10.160
And then with this
kind of information,

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:12.202
we can roll it up to any
time period of interest.

00:31:12.202 --> 00:31:14.300
So you could do, for
example, a few week period

00:31:14.300 --> 00:31:16.260
before a light change,
change the light timing,

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:17.510
and then look at a
few week period after.

00:31:17.510 --> 00:31:19.100
And measure if there
were any differences.

00:31:19.100 --> 00:31:20.600
And doing this throughout
the entire city

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:21.740
and looking at the
network effects

00:31:21.740 --> 00:31:22.910
upstream and downstream,
you can really

00:31:22.910 --> 00:31:25.310
start to optimize, and
shave off those few seconds

00:31:25.310 --> 00:31:27.380
that we were talking about
that gives people back

00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:29.330
time and money of their day.

00:31:29.330 --> 00:31:32.120
And really helps reduce
the environmental impact

00:31:32.120 --> 00:31:34.500
of idling and congestion.

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:36.650
So that ends the tech
portion of our talk here.

00:31:36.650 --> 00:31:38.672
And I want to jump
into a Q&amp;A with Ryan.

00:31:38.672 --> 00:31:40.880
So he's scripted some
questions that he didn't really

00:31:40.880 --> 00:31:41.922
share with me beforehand.

00:31:41.922 --> 00:31:43.460
He wanted it to
be a real live Q&amp;A

00:31:43.460 --> 00:31:45.585
to give you the feel of
actually being with us here

00:31:45.585 --> 00:31:46.982
as an audience.

00:31:46.982 --> 00:31:47.690
RYAN LIPPERT: OK.

00:31:47.690 --> 00:31:49.070
Thanks Daniel,
and thanks so much

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:50.278
for sharing everything there.

00:31:50.278 --> 00:31:53.450
The stuff that you and Geotab
are doing is really impressive.

00:31:53.450 --> 00:31:55.790
And I think, as you mentioned,
it was a little bit more

00:31:55.790 --> 00:31:58.250
on the technical side by
design, the conversation

00:31:58.250 --> 00:32:00.093
that you just had there
with the listeners.

00:32:00.093 --> 00:32:02.510
Can you talk a little bit more
about what the benefits are

00:32:02.510 --> 00:32:05.315
on the business side for your
customers and how that works?

00:32:05.315 --> 00:32:06.780
DANIEL LEWIS: Yeah, absolutely.

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:09.798
So I mean, this impacts
of this particular project

00:32:09.798 --> 00:32:10.590
that we worked on--

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:11.390
I mean, we do a
lot of other things

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:14.128
as well-- but I'd also just
say that a lot of our customers

00:32:14.128 --> 00:32:16.670
actually are cities, and have
their municipal vehicles hooked

00:32:16.670 --> 00:32:18.403
up with our IoT device.

00:32:18.403 --> 00:32:19.820
So that being said
though, there's

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:21.487
a lot of impacts for
our fleet customers

00:32:21.487 --> 00:32:22.980
as well from
something like this.

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:24.500
So if you're going to do
heavy duty vehicle driving

00:32:24.500 --> 00:32:26.540
around the city, every
time you have to stop,

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:27.745
that idling uses up fuel.

00:32:27.745 --> 00:32:29.870
It also takes a lot longer
for a heavy duty vehicle

00:32:29.870 --> 00:32:31.100
to accelerate again.

00:32:31.100 --> 00:32:34.052
That uses up fuel and driver
time and things like that.

00:32:34.052 --> 00:32:35.510
And then from a
city's perspective,

00:32:35.510 --> 00:32:38.150
when that big truck has to
stop and then accelerate again,

00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:39.300
that causes congestion.

00:32:39.300 --> 00:32:42.050
So it's a win for the city by
reducing congestion, reducing

00:32:42.050 --> 00:32:43.800
emissions, idling,
things like that.

00:32:43.800 --> 00:32:45.710
But it's also just a
win for our customers

00:32:45.710 --> 00:32:47.930
in having to spend less
gas and waiting less time.

00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:49.860
And when you think about
one individual truck,

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:51.667
it maybe doesn't seem
that big of a deal.

00:32:51.667 --> 00:32:53.750
But if you think back to
the San Francisco example

00:32:53.750 --> 00:32:55.850
with 450,000 cars-- so
most of our customers

00:32:55.850 --> 00:32:57.433
don't have that many,
but some of them

00:32:57.433 --> 00:33:00.440
definitely do have 5, 10,
20,000 vehicles-- if you imagine

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:02.970
these little stops constantly
across all these cities, when

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:05.577
you've got 10,000, 20,000
trucks driving every single day,

00:33:05.577 --> 00:33:07.160
these little
improvements, they really

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:09.260
add up if you can make
those small changes.

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:12.165
And then speaking with certain
cities, so you saw on the map

00:33:12.165 --> 00:33:14.540
before of Las Vegas, there
are some corridors that trucks

00:33:14.540 --> 00:33:15.523
like to travel down.

00:33:15.523 --> 00:33:17.690
And a city, once they have
this kind of information,

00:33:17.690 --> 00:33:19.815
can start to think about
things like freight signal

00:33:19.815 --> 00:33:20.620
prioritization.

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:22.710
So because these trucks take
a lot longer to accelerate,

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:24.127
there's a larger
congestion impact

00:33:24.127 --> 00:33:25.310
from them having to stop.

00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:27.890
So if you're a city who
wanted to prioritize freight

00:33:27.890 --> 00:33:30.470
along a certain corridor and
minimize the congestion impact,

00:33:30.470 --> 00:33:32.530
you can do things like give an
extra couple seconds of green

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:34.530
when a truck is approaching
to let it go through.

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:35.720
So obviously, this is
a win for the customer

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:37.387
because they don't
have to stop as much.

00:33:37.387 --> 00:33:40.380
And it's a win for the city not
having to reduce congestion.

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:40.860
RYAN LIPPERT: I
don't think there's

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:42.180
any of us who
wouldn't rather have

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:43.847
less congestion on
the streets, and so I

00:33:43.847 --> 00:33:46.200
think that's a win for just
about everyone out there.

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:48.720
You mentioned that you're
staying within Google Cloud

00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:50.550
as you do this
and it's BigQuery,

00:33:50.550 --> 00:33:52.440
there's some Dataflow in there.

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:54.630
Can you help describe
to our listeners

00:33:54.630 --> 00:33:57.510
out there what it would take
if you didn't have BigQuery

00:33:57.510 --> 00:33:59.168
and the surrounding tools?

00:33:59.168 --> 00:34:00.460
DANIEL LEWIS: Yeah, absolutely.

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:01.710
So I think a lot
of it would be what

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:03.002
you mentioned at the beginning.

00:34:03.002 --> 00:34:05.190
If we didn't have all of
this amazing infrastructure

00:34:05.190 --> 00:34:07.830
on the backend to do the scaling
and manage all that for us,

00:34:07.830 --> 00:34:10.389
we'd have to spend a lot
more time in that gray area,

00:34:10.389 --> 00:34:11.550
and actually managing
and building out

00:34:11.550 --> 00:34:12.342
the infrastructure.

00:34:12.342 --> 00:34:14.790
And that's not really where
our data scientist skills

00:34:14.790 --> 00:34:16.889
or expertise lies,
at least not for me.

00:34:16.889 --> 00:34:19.906
My expertise and my value add
is in solving these business

00:34:19.906 --> 00:34:20.739
problems for people.

00:34:20.739 --> 00:34:22.830
And if I had to spend
80%, 90% of my time

00:34:22.830 --> 00:34:24.659
doing out the infrastructure,
the installation probably

00:34:24.659 --> 00:34:26.429
wouldn't be as good because
I can't devote as much time

00:34:26.429 --> 00:34:26.699
to it.

00:34:26.699 --> 00:34:28.290
So what we probably
would have done then

00:34:28.290 --> 00:34:29.582
is using the tools that we had.

00:34:29.582 --> 00:34:31.770
If we didn't have all this
amazing ability to scale,

00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:33.120
just really reduce
the scope down.

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:35.662
We maybe would have had to do
it neighborhood by neighborhood

00:34:35.662 --> 00:34:37.239
or maybe city by
city, and come up

00:34:37.239 --> 00:34:39.790
with other kind of limitations
to restrict the amount of data

00:34:39.790 --> 00:34:41.969
that's going in just because we
wouldn't be able to handle it.

00:34:41.969 --> 00:34:43.170
But because we're able
to, we're able to do

00:34:43.170 --> 00:34:45.150
this globally,
which helps us to be

00:34:45.150 --> 00:34:48.760
a much more powerful product.

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:50.179
RYAN LIPPERT:
Makes sense Daniel.

