And if you're not
quite there yet,

00:35:27.740 --> 00:35:29.560
BigQuery has got a lot of
sample data sets in there

00:35:29.560 --> 00:35:30.970
and you can play
around with those.

00:35:30.970 --> 00:35:32.428
But really need to
start digging in

00:35:32.428 --> 00:35:34.000
and applying it
to actual problems

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:36.252
to understand the new
techniques, the new tools,

00:35:36.252 --> 00:35:37.210
and their capabilities.

00:35:37.210 --> 00:35:39.190
And once you do, it's
almost always the case

00:35:39.190 --> 00:35:41.440
that you can really quickly
implement it in production

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:44.110
and get some really big
wins, like we did with BQGIS

00:35:44.110 --> 00:35:46.155
and like we did with
SQL scripting as well.

00:35:46.155 --> 00:35:48.280
And Google's also got some
lots of great champions,

00:35:48.280 --> 00:35:50.572
like Fullybay and [INAUDIBLE],,
and that are constantly

00:35:50.572 --> 00:35:53.650
pushing out content about
how to use these new tools

00:35:53.650 --> 00:35:54.590
with examples given.

00:35:54.590 --> 00:35:56.590
And if you can just go
and replicate their work,

00:35:56.590 --> 00:35:58.210
start making tweaks, thinking
about how to apply it

00:35:58.210 --> 00:35:59.627
to your own data,
you can actually

00:35:59.627 --> 00:36:02.230
get ramped up pretty quickly
with all of this stuff.

00:36:02.230 --> 00:36:03.886
That would be my advice.

00:36:03.886 --> 00:36:05.310
RYAN LIPPERT:
Daniel, great advice

00:36:05.310 --> 00:36:07.020
to go along with a
great presentation.

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:09.600
I can't thank you enough
for coming and sharing

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:12.480
your thoughts today and
what you're doing at Geotab.

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:14.250
And just wish both
you and Geotab

00:36:14.250 --> 00:36:16.090
the best of luck going forward.

00:36:16.090 --> 00:36:18.050
DANIEL LEWIS: Thanks Ryan.

